**(2) Root causes of each symptom**

- **Immediate overdue:** confirmed in an in-memory replay of the actual resolver: before trigger → “Next in 1 min”; at trigger/+1 second → “Overdue”; snooze snapshot supplied → “Rings again”; snooze expires → original overdue time returns. A daily repeater instead showed tomorrow at its trigger. The implementation has neither active-occurrence identity nor ringing input. [lib/remindersMembership.ts:22](/C:/Dev/VR/lib/remindersMembership.ts:22), [lib/remindersMembership.ts:43](/C:/Dev/VR/lib/remindersMembership.ts:43)
- **Later updates late:** foreground reconciliation and the card refresh run independently. The card can read storage before reconciliation writes it, then wait another tick. If an intent runs while JS remains foregrounded without an AppState transition, the tick only rereads AsyncStorage—it does **not** drain native events. Therefore the delay is not necessarily bounded by 30 seconds. [app/_layout.tsx:108](/C:/Dev/VR/app/_layout.tsx:108), [app/index.tsx:337](/C:/Dev/VR/app/index.tsx:337), [lib/notifications.ts:1966](/C:/Dev/VR/lib/notifications.ts:1966)
- **Later can display the wrong return time:** native code records `tap time + 5 minutes`, but normally retains the prearmed comeback at `original occurrence + 5/10/15 minutes`. At the cap, it can record a snooze even though follow-up scheduling returns without arming anything. Fallback snoozes also never populate the card’s AlarmKit-only snooze mirror. [plugins/ios-src/VRAlarmIntents.swift:525](/C:/Dev/VR/plugins/ios-src/VRAlarmIntents.swift:525), [plugins/ios-src/VRAlarmIntents.swift:549](/C:/Dev/VR/plugins/ios-src/VRAlarmIntents.swift:549), [plugins/ios-src/VRAlarmIntents.swift:593](/C:/Dev/VR/plugins/ios-src/VRAlarmIntents.swift:593), [lib/alarmKit.ts:346](/C:/Dev/VR/lib/alarmKit.ts:346)
- **The X:** our AlarmOverlay and alarm screen already show checkmark **Done**. AlarmKit also receives that configuration, but **iOS 26.1+ ignores the custom Stop appearance and supplies its own control**. On the authorized AlarmKit route, the reported X is therefore most consistent with the system Stop control, mapped to `VRStopIntent`; its completion reaches the card late through the path above. A plain notification dismissal is different: its handler nags rather than completes. Identifying the owner’s exact surface remains unverified. [components/AlarmOverlay.tsx:577](/C:/Dev/VR/components/AlarmOverlay.tsx:577), [app/alarm.tsx:445](/C:/Dev/VR/app/alarm.tsx:445), [plugins/withAlarmKit.js:407](/C:/Dev/VR/plugins/withAlarmKit.js:407), [lib/notifications.ts:2922](/C:/Dev/VR/lib/notifications.ts:2922); [Apple Stop-button documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/alarmkit/alarmpresentation/alert-swift.struct/stopbutton).
- **“Timeout” is currently an assumption on AlarmKit.** The 180-second constant only classifies `fired` events; native code does not currently produce those events or observe `alarmUpdates`. `getScheduledAlarms()` returns our registry, not actual daemon states. Consequently, neither elapsed time nor that registry proves ringing ended. [lib/alarmKit.ts:463](/C:/Dev/VR/lib/alarmKit.ts:463), [plugins/withAlarmKit.js:96](/C:/Dev/VR/plugins/withAlarmKit.js:96), [docs/cl4-escalation-research.md:17](/C:/Dev/VR/docs/cl4-escalation-research.md:17)

**(3) Interaction matrix**

“Card during” means its current computation when visible/reopened: **one-off overdue; repeater next grid occurrence**, unless a live snooze snapshot overrides it. Locked/background cards are not visibly updating. [lib/remindersMembership.ts:19](/C:/Dev/VR/lib/remindersMembership.ts:19)

